      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you give me a summary of the competing legal philosophies regarding the interpretation of the Second Amendment, specifically focusing on the tension between originalism and living constitutionalism? I'm looking for the core arguments each side uses to justify their reading of the text.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalism is a conservative, Constitution-obsessed approach that views the Second Amendment as a fixed, unchanging guarantee of individual right to bear arms, rooted in the 18th-century English common law and the Founding Fathers' intentions. They argue that the text's plain meaning and historical context should be used to determine its original scope and applicability. In contrast, living constitutionalism is a liberal, evolving approach that sees the Second Amendment as a dynamic, context-dependent right that must be interpreted in light of contemporary societal values and changing circumstances. They contend that the text's meaning should be reevaluated and expanded to reflect modern understandings of individual liberty and self-defense.",
